<Base title={'Ommisa CLI — documentation'} description={'Install and use the Ommisa CLI: login, questions, memory files, conversations, projects, and configuration.'}>
<h1>Ommisa CLI</h1>
<p class="lede">The Ommisa CLI runs on Node.js 18 and above and carries no runtime dependencies. The answer streams to the terminal as it is written; standard output carries only the reply, while the spinner, warnings and quota notes are written to standard error.</p>

<h2>Installation</h2>
<pre><code>npm install -g @ommisa/cli</code></pre>

<h2>Signing in</h2>
<pre><code>ommisa login
ommisa login --read-only</code></pre>
<p>The login uses the OAuth Device Authorization Grant (RFC 8628) against the OIML SMART identity service. The command prints a short code and opens the approval page; approving the code completes the sign-in. The credentials are stored at <code>~/.config/ommisa/credentials.json</code> with mode 0600. The identity service issues a personal access token scoped with the <code>&lt;service&gt;:&lt;read|write|admin&gt;</code> grammar, and it re-judges the token on every request, so revocation ends access within seconds.</p>

<h2>Asking questions</h2>
<pre><code>ommisa ask "What is the MPE requirement for a load cell per OIML R 60?"
ommisa ask --lang fr "Qu'est-ce que l'EMT d'une cellule de charge ?"
ommisa ask --fresh "..."
ommisa ask --public "..."
ommisa ask "..." --save
ommisa ask "..." --conversation &lt;id&gt;
ommisa ask -c "..."                       # continue the last stored thread
ommisa ask "..." --attach nameplate.png   # an image rides the question
cat report.md | ommisa ask "Summarize this against R 60."</code></pre>
<p>The command uses the member tier when the machine holds credentials and the public tier otherwise; the tier, the remaining quota and the sign-in instruction are printed with every answer. The reply is the only thing written to standard output; the spinner, the citations and the warnings ride standard error, so redirection and pipes stay clean. The <code>--public</code> option forces the anonymous lane even when credentials exist. The <code>--fresh</code> option bypasses the answer caches, the <code>--save</code> option stores the turn as a new conversation, <code>--conversation</code> continues a stored conversation, and <code>-c</code> continues the most recent one. The <code>--attach</code> option puts a PNG, JPEG, WebP or GIF image (at most 4 MB) on the question — a photo is seen only in the turn it accompanies. Piped standard input becomes the question's context.</p>

<h2>A thread in the terminal</h2>
<pre><code>ommisa chat</code></pre>
<p>The chat command holds one conversation in the terminal: every follow-up rides the thread's history. Slash commands manage it — <code>/new</code> clears the thread, <code>/attach FILE</code> puts an image on the next question, <code>/lang LL</code> switches the answer language, <code>/save</code> stores the thread to your account, and <code>/exit</code> leaves. Markdown answers are rendered for the terminal when standard output is a screen.</p>

<h2>Composing with other tools</h2>
<pre><code>ommisa ask "..." --json        # the full response document on stdout
ommisa ask "..." --stream-json # NDJSON events: stage, token, done
ommisa ask "..." -o answer.md  # the markdown reply to a file
ommisa log [N]                 # the last asks from this machine's log</code></pre>
<p>The <code>--json</code> option prints the machine-readable response — the answer, its citations, the source quality, the confidence note and the quota — for anything that parses. The <code>--stream-json</code> option emits one JSON object per line as the answer progresses, ending with a <code>done</code> event that carries the complete payload. The exit code is a contract: 0 for a delivered answer, 1 for a failure, 2 for a usage error, 4 for a spent quota, 130 for an interruption. Every ask is appended to a local log that <code>ommisa log</code> reads.</p>

<h2>Memory files, conversations and projects</h2>
<pre><code>ommisa memories
ommisa memories add "My laboratory" "Class C load cells at 23 degrees Celsius."
ommisa memories rm &lt;id&gt;
ommisa conversations
ommisa conversations show &lt;id&gt;
ommisa conversations new "Load cells"
ommisa conversations rm &lt;id&gt;
ommisa files
ommisa files &lt;project-id&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Memory files that the account holder enables join subsequent questions as context, on the web application and in the CLI alike.</p>

<h2>Session and configuration</h2>
<pre><code>ommisa status
ommisa whoami
ommisa logout</code></pre>
<p>The environment variables <code>OMMISA_ID</code>, <code>OMMISA_API</code>, <code>OMMISA_CLIENT_ID</code> and <code>OMMISA_SCOPE</code> override the defaults, and the flags <code>--issuer</code>, <code>--api</code>, <code>--client-id</code> and <code>--scope</code> override them per call.</p>

<h2>Security posture</h2>
<p>The CLI is a registered public client and holds no secret. It requests read and write scopes for the <code>oiml-ai</code> service, and the identity service judges the request against the approving account's standing at approval and at every renewal. The AI service admits the token as a read credential and refuses writes unless the approved scope states a write.</p>
